{"name":"Blinking Blue","hex":"#0033ff","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blinking","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That blinks.","bloody"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of blink"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blinking"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blinking","blocks":[{"text":"Blinking is a bodily function; it is a semi-autonomic rapid closing of the eyelid. A single blink consists of the closing of the eyelid or inactivation of the levator palpebrae superioris and the activation of the palpebral portion of the orbicularis oculi. It is an essential function of the eye that helps spread tears across and remove irritants from the surface of the cornea and conjunctiva."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blinking","wikidata":"Q1044679"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Eye_opening_and_closing.gif/250px-Eye_opening_and_closing.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Eye_opening_and_closing.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Eye_opening_and_closing.gif/250px-Eye_opening_and_closing.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Human eye blinking","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEye_opening_and_closing.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Bird_blink-edit.jpg/500px-Bird_blink-edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Bird_blink-edit.jpg/1280px-Bird_blink-edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Bird_blink-edit.jpg/500px-Bird_blink-edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Birds, reptiles and sharks blink with a nictitating membrane from one side of the eye to the other.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABird_blink-edit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Closed_human_eye%2C_superior_view.jpg/500px-Closed_human_eye%2C_superior_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Closed_human_eye%2C_superior_view.jpg/1280px-Closed_human_eye%2C_superior_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&u